window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["angular"]=[{"id":"angular-f0001","card":"q-angular-001-2-angular-overview-versioning-license-to","sec":"angular-s003","q":"Why is Angular described as a platform and not only a UI library?","a":"<p>Angular ships the pieces an application needs in one coherent package: a component model, templates, routing, forms, an HTTP client, dependency injection, testing tools and a CLI. A UI library such as React covers the view layer and leaves routing, state and data fetching to other libraries. The trade-off is that Angular is more opinionated and has a larger learning curve, but teams get one consistent structure for large applications.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0002","card":"q-angular-001-2-angular-overview-versioning-license-to","sec":"angular-s003","q":"What is a single-page application (SPA), and how does Angular implement one?","a":"<p>An SPA loads one HTML page and then updates the content in the browser without a full page reload. Angular mounts the root component into the page, and its router swaps components in and out as the URL changes, while data comes from HTTP calls. The benefit is a fast, app-like feel. The costs are a heavier first load and extra work for SEO, which server-side rendering can address.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0003","card":"q-angular-001-2-angular-overview-versioning-license-to","sec":"angular-s003","q":"How does Angular's approach to templates differ from a framework that mixes markup into JavaScript?","a":"<p>Angular keeps the HTML template in its own file or string and extends HTML with bindings, directives and pipes. The behavior lives in a TypeScript class. Designers can read the markup, and tools can check templates at compile time. Frameworks that write markup inside JavaScript (JSX) give more flexibility in code, but the logic and the view are mixed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0004","card":"q-angular-002-release-and-support-semver","sec":"angular-s004","q":"In Angular's semantic versioning, what do major, minor and patch releases mean?","a":"<p>Major releases can contain big features and breaking changes, with an upgrade path. Minor releases add smaller features and stay backward compatible. Patch releases fix bugs and security issues and are low risk, so you should apply them quickly. Under SemVer, only a major bump is allowed to break your code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0005","card":"q-angular-002-release-and-support-semver","sec":"angular-s004","q":"What is the release cadence and the support window for an Angular major version?","a":"<p>As of Angular's recent release policy, a major version comes about every 6 months. Each major gets 6 months of active support (features, minor and patch releases) and 12 months of long-term support (LTS, patches only), 18 months in total. Check the official release page for the current policy, because the schedule and support windows have been adjusted over time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0006","card":"q-angular-002-release-and-support-semver","sec":"angular-s004","q":"How do you upgrade an Angular application to a newer major version, and why upgrade one major at a time?","a":"<p>Run <code>ng update @angular/core @angular/cli</code>, which updates the packages and runs schematics that migrate your code automatically. Going one major at a time keeps each migration small, uses the official migration steps for that version, and makes it easier to find which change broke something. All <code>@angular/*</code> packages share the same major version, and the CLI major matches the framework major.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0007","card":"q-angular-002-release-and-support-semver","sec":"angular-s004","q":"Why does a team that cannot upgrade immediately still care about the LTS window?","a":"<p>During LTS the version only receives critical fixes, such as security patches. After LTS ends, there are no fixes at all, so known vulnerabilities stay open. Teams plan upgrades so they never run an unsupported major in production.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0008","card":"q-angular-003-mit-license","sec":"angular-s005","q":"What does it mean for a company that Angular is MIT licensed?","a":"<p>The MIT license is permissive. You can use, copy, modify and distribute Angular, including in commercial and closed-source products. The only obligation is to keep the copyright notice and license text. There is no warranty, and the authors are not liable. Unlike GPL, it does not force you to open-source your own code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0009","card":"q-angular-004-angular-cli-ng","sec":"angular-s006","q":"What problems does the Angular CLI solve?","a":"<p>It creates projects with a working build setup, generates components, services and other files with the right structure, runs the dev server, builds optimized production bundles, runs tests and linting, and upgrades the app with <code>ng update</code>. This keeps projects consistent across a team and removes manual build configuration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0010","card":"q-angular-004-angular-cli-ng","sec":"angular-s006","q":"Which CLI commands do you use for the daily loop of building, serving, testing and linting an Angular app?","a":"<p><code>ng serve</code> runs the development server with live reload. <code>ng build</code> compiles the app, and a production build is the default for <code>ng build</code> in recent versions. <code>ng test</code> runs the unit tests. <code>ng lint</code> runs the linter (it needs a linter package installed, which the CLI offers to add). <code>ng generate</code> (alias <code>ng g</code>) creates files, and <code>ng update</code> upgrades dependencies.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0011","card":"q-angular-004-angular-cli-ng","sec":"angular-s006","q":"In an Angular workspace, how would you create a component, a service and a pipe with the CLI?","a":"<p><code>ng generate component name</code>, <code>ng generate service name</code> and <code>ng generate pipe name</code>. Each command creates the class file with its decorator and a spec file, and for components also the template and style files. For a component, the CLI also updates the declaring module when the app is module based.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0012","card":"q-angular-004-angular-cli-ng","sec":"angular-s006","q":"Why do many teams run <code>npm start</code> or <code>npm test</code> instead of calling <code>ng</code> directly?","a":"<p>The npm scripts in <code>package.json</code> use the CLI version installed in the project. A global <code>ng</code> can be a different version from the project's, which causes confusing differences. Scripts also give the team one shared set of flags.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0013","card":"q-angular-005-developer-tools","sec":"angular-s007","q":"What are Angular schematics, and how do they relate to <code>ng generate</code> and <code>ng update</code>?","a":"<p>Schematics are code transformations that create or modify files in a project. <code>ng generate</code> runs schematics to scaffold components, services and so on. <code>ng update</code> runs migration schematics to rewrite your code for a new version. Libraries can ship their own schematics, for example to add themselves to a project with <code>ng add</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0014","card":"q-angular-005-developer-tools","sec":"angular-s007","q":"What does Angular DevTools give you that browser developer tools alone do not?","a":"<p>Angular DevTools is a browser extension that shows the component tree with inputs and state, lets you inspect and edit component properties, and includes a profiler that shows how long change detection takes per component. Plain browser tools only show the rendered DOM.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0015","card":"q-angular-005-developer-tools","sec":"angular-s007","q":"What is the Angular Language Service, and why install it?","a":"<p>It is an editor plugin that understands Angular templates. It gives autocomplete, type checking of bindings, go-to-definition and error hints inside templates, which normally are just strings or HTML files to an editor.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0016","card":"q-angular-006-angular-json","sec":"angular-s008","q":"What is the role of <code>angular.json</code> in an Angular workspace?","a":"<p>It is the CLI workspace configuration. It lists the projects in the workspace, and for each project it defines build targets (build, serve, test, lint) with the builder to use, the options such as entry file, output path and TypeScript config, and named configurations such as <code>production</code> and <code>development</code>. The CLI reads it for every <code>ng</code> command.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0017","card":"q-angular-006-angular-json","sec":"angular-s008","q":"In <code>angular.json</code>, what is the purpose of the <code>configurations</code> section of a build target, and how do you choose one?","a":"<p>A configuration holds option overrides for a named scenario, for example production sets optimization and output hashing on, and development turns optimization off and source maps on. Select it with <code>ng build --configuration production</code>, or set a <code>defaultConfiguration</code> for the target. This keeps one build definition with different settings per environment.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0018","card":"q-angular-006-angular-json","sec":"angular-s008","q":"Why does a production Angular build use <code>outputHashing</code>?","a":"<p>With <code>outputHashing</code> set to <code>all</code>, the build puts a content hash in each output file name. When the content changes, the name changes, so browsers and CDNs fetch the new file, while unchanged files stay cached for a long time. This enables aggressive caching without stale files.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0019","card":"q-angular-006-angular-json","sec":"angular-s008","q":"Can one Angular workspace hold several applications or libraries, and how is that configured?","a":"<p>Yes. The <code>projects</code> section of <code>angular.json</code> has one entry per application or library, each with its own build targets. You create them with <code>ng generate application</code> or <code>ng generate library</code>, and a shared library can then be used by several applications in the same repository.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0020","card":"q-angular-007-decorators","sec":"angular-s009","q":"What is a decorator in Angular, and why can you not build an Angular app without them?","a":"<p>A decorator is a function applied with the <code>@Name()</code> syntax that attaches metadata to a class or member. Angular reads that metadata to know what a class is (component, directive, pipe, module, injectable service) and how to compile and inject it. Without it a class is just a plain TypeScript class.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0021","card":"q-angular-007-decorators","sec":"angular-s009","q":"What is wrong with each of these decorators: <code>@Component</code>, <code>@Component()</code> and <code>@Component({});</code>?","a":"<p><code>@Component</code> has no parentheses, so the decorator function is not called. <code>@Component()</code> is called with no configuration, but a component needs at least a template (and normally a selector). <code>@Component({});</code> has a semicolon after the decorator, which is a syntax error because the decorator must be followed by the class. Current Angular reports these at compile time; older versions could fail at runtime.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0022","card":"q-angular-007-decorators","sec":"angular-s009","q":"Which decorators does Angular provide for classes, and which for properties?","a":"<p>Class decorators: <code>@Component</code>, <code>@Directive</code>, <code>@Pipe</code>, <code>@Injectable</code> and <code>@NgModule</code>. Property decorators include <code>@Input</code> (receive data from a parent), <code>@Output</code> (emit events to a parent, usually an <code>EventEmitter</code>), <code>@HostListener</code> and <code>@HostBinding</code> (listen to or bind host element events and properties), and <code>@ViewChild</code> and <code>@ContentChild</code> (query children).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0023","card":"q-angular-007-decorators","sec":"angular-s009","q":"What is the difference between <code>@Input() name</code> and <code>@Input('alias') name</code>?","a":"<p>Without an alias, the binding uses the property name: <code>[name]=\"value\"</code>. With an alias, the template uses the alias, <code>[alias]=\"value\"</code>, while the class still uses <code>name</code>. Aliases help when a directive input should share its selector name, but renaming public inputs breaks consumers, so use them sparingly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0024","card":"q-angular-008-modules-ngmodule","sec":"angular-s010","q":"What is an NgModule, and what problem does it solve?","a":"<p>An NgModule groups related components, directives, pipes and services, and tells the compiler what is available inside the templates of its components. It organizes an app into cohesive blocks, supports lazy loading of feature areas, and controls what is shared through <code>exports</code>. The class body is empty, and the decorator metadata does the work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0025","card":"q-angular-008-modules-ngmodule","sec":"angular-s010","q":"In <code>@NgModule</code>, what do <code>declarations</code>, <code>imports</code>, <code>exports</code>, <code>providers</code> and <code>bootstrap</code> each do?","a":"<p><code>declarations</code>: the components, directives and pipes this module owns. <code>imports</code>: other modules whose exported items the templates here can use. <code>exports</code>: the items this module makes available to modules that import it. <code>providers</code>: services registered for dependency injection. <code>bootstrap</code>: the root component(s) to start, only in the root module.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0026","card":"q-angular-008-modules-ngmodule","sec":"angular-s010","q":"Why can a component be declared in only one NgModule, and what happens if you declare it in two?","a":"<p>The compiler needs one module to decide which other directives and pipes are visible in the component's template. If it is declared in two modules, the template context is ambiguous, so the compiler raises an error. To share a component, declare it in one module and add it to that module's <code>exports</code>, then import the module elsewhere.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0027","card":"q-angular-008-modules-ngmodule","sec":"angular-s010","q":"Where does a service go in an NgModule, and where does a module go?","a":"<p>Modules go in <code>imports</code>. Services go in <code>providers</code>. Putting a module in <code>providers</code> or a service in <code>imports</code> is a common mistake. With <code>@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })</code>, a service needs no <code>providers</code> entry at all.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0028","card":"q-angular-008-modules-ngmodule","sec":"angular-s010","q":"What is the root module compared to a feature module, and why split an app into feature modules?","a":"<p>The root module (usually <code>AppModule</code>) is the only one with <code>bootstrap</code>, and it starts the app. Feature modules hold one area of the app, such as users or orders. Splitting keeps code organized and allows lazy loading, so the browser downloads a feature only when the user visits it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0029","card":"q-angular-008-modules-ngmodule","sec":"angular-s010","q":"In a module-based app, which function starts the application, and what does it take as arguments?","a":"<p><code>platformBrowserDynamic().bootstrapModule(AppModule)</code> in <code>main.ts</code>. It takes the root module and an optional options object for global settings such as view encapsulation. In standalone apps, <code>bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, config)</code> plays the same role.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0030","card":"q-angular-009-4-components","sec":"angular-s011","q":"What are the four parts of an Angular component, and how do they work together?","a":"<p>A template (the HTML), styles (CSS), a TypeScript class (state and behavior) and a selector (the custom element name that places it in another template or the DOM). The class holds data and methods, and the template binds to them. The selector is how other templates use the component.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0031","card":"q-angular-009-4-components","sec":"angular-s011","q":"What is the difference between a routed page component and a reusable component, and does Angular treat them differently?","a":"<p>Angular treats them identically, since both are classes with <code>@Component</code>. The difference is how they are used. A page component is reached by a route and rendered in <code>&lt;router-outlet&gt;</code>, while a reusable component is placed in templates through its selector. Teams often keep them in separate folders to make that role clear.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0032","card":"q-angular-009-4-components","sec":"angular-s011","q":"In a component class, why should a service be injected through the constructor instead of created with <code>new</code>?","a":"<p>Constructor injection lets Angular supply the instance from its injector, so the component shares a singleton where needed and does not depend on how the service is built. It also lets tests replace the service with a fake. Creating it with <code>new</code> couples the component to that class and breaks sharing and testing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0033","card":"q-angular-010-decorator-properties","sec":"angular-s012","q":"Why must a component selector contain a dash, and what prefix convention is used?","a":"<p>Standard HTML element names have no dash, and custom elements must contain one, so a dash in the selector prevents clashes with current and future HTML elements. The convention is a project prefix plus kebab-case, such as <code>app-user-list</code>. A lint rule can enforce the prefix.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0034","card":"q-angular-010-decorator-properties","sec":"angular-s012","q":"What is the difference between <code>templateUrl</code> and <code>template</code>, and can you use both?","a":"<p><code>templateUrl</code> points to an external HTML file and <code>template</code> holds inline HTML. You can use only one of them on a component. External files are recommended for anything beyond a line or two, because editors and designers handle them better. Inline templates suit tiny components.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0035","card":"q-angular-010-decorator-properties","sec":"angular-s012","q":"What is the difference between <code>styleUrls</code> and <code>styles</code> in <code>@Component</code>?","a":"<p><code>styleUrls</code> lists external CSS files and <code>styles</code> takes an array of inline CSS strings. They can be combined. In Angular 17 and later there is also a single <code>styleUrl</code> property for one file.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0036","card":"q-angular-010-decorator-properties","sec":"angular-s012","q":"What does the <code>encapsulation</code> property do, and what is the default?","a":"<p>It controls how component styles are scoped. The default, <code>Emulated</code>, adds unique attributes to selectors so styles apply only to that component's template, without using the browser's Shadow DOM. <code>ShadowDom</code> uses native Shadow DOM, so global CSS no longer reaches the component. <code>None</code> makes the styles global.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0037","card":"q-angular-011-template-reference-variable","sec":"angular-s013","q":"What is a template reference variable in Angular, and when do you use one?","a":"<p>A name prefixed with <code>#</code> on an element, such as <code>#filter</code>. It gives the template direct access to that element (or component) so you can read its properties, like <code>filter.value</code>, or pass them to a handler, without extra class state. Use it for simple cases such as reading an input on click.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0038","card":"q-angular-011-template-reference-variable","sec":"angular-s013","q":"What is the scope of a template reference variable?","a":"<p>It is available in the whole template where it is declared, including child elements and embedded templates created inside it, but not from the component class. To use it in the class, pass it to a method call, or query it with <code>@ViewChild</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0039","card":"q-angular-011-template-reference-variable","sec":"angular-s013","q":"How would you read the value of an input box when a button is clicked, using the template only?","a":"<p>Put a reference on the input and pass its value to a handler:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">HTML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>&lt;input #box type=&quot;text&quot;&gt;\n&lt;button (click)=&quot;search(box.value)&quot;&gt;Search&lt;/button&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>box</code> is the input element, so <code>box.value</code> is the text. This avoids two-way binding or forms when you only need the value at click time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0040","card":"q-angular-012-three-binding-types","sec":"angular-s014","q":"What are the three binding types in Angular templates, and which direction does data flow in each?","a":"<p>Interpolation <code>{{ expr }}</code> and property binding <code>[prop]=\"expr\"</code> send data from the class to the view. Event binding <code>(event)=\"handler()\"</code> sends events from the view to the class. Two-way binding <code>[(ngModel)]</code> combines property and event binding.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0041","card":"q-angular-012-three-binding-types","sec":"angular-s014","q":"When do you use property binding instead of interpolation?","a":"<p>Interpolation converts a value to a string and shows it as text or inside an attribute string. Property binding sets a DOM property or a component input with its real type, so you can pass objects, booleans or arrays. For example, <code>[disabled]=\"isBusy\"</code> sets a boolean, and <code>[appStatus]=\"user.online\"</code> passes data to a directive input.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0042","card":"q-angular-012-three-binding-types","sec":"angular-s014","q":"What is the difference between <code>&lt;img src=\"{{ url }}\"&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;img [src]=\"url\"&gt;</code>?","a":"<p>They give the same result for strings, because interpolation in an attribute is shorthand for a property binding with string concatenation. Use <code>[src]</code> for a single value, which keeps its type. Use interpolation when mixing text and values, such as <code>src=\"/img/{{ name }}.png\"</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0043","card":"q-angular-012-three-binding-types","sec":"angular-s014","q":"In a template, what runs when you write <code>(click)=\"update(''); filter.value = ''\"</code>?","a":"<p>Angular runs both statements, separated by a semicolon, in order on each click: it calls <code>update('')</code> on the component, then assigns an empty string to the reference variable's <code>value</code>, which clears the input. Multiple statements are allowed in an event binding, but long logic belongs in a method.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0044","card":"q-angular-013-structural-directives","sec":"angular-s015","q":"What makes a directive structural, and what does the <code>*</code> prefix mean?","a":"<p>A structural directive changes the DOM structure by adding or removing elements, and the <code>*</code> is shorthand that Angular expands into an <code>&lt;ng-template&gt;</code> wrapped around the element. <code>*ngIf</code> and <code>*ngFor</code> are the common built-in ones. They remove the elements from the DOM, they do not hide them with CSS.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0045","card":"q-angular-013-structural-directives","sec":"angular-s015","q":"What is the difference between <code>*ngIf=\"cond\"</code> and <code>[hidden]=\"!cond\"</code>?","a":"<p><code>*ngIf</code> removes the element and its component tree from the DOM when false, and creates it again when true, so it frees memory and runs init and destroy hooks. <code>[hidden]</code> keeps the element in the DOM and only hides it, which is cheaper to toggle often but keeps everything alive.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0046","card":"q-angular-013-structural-directives","sec":"angular-s015","q":"How does <code>*ngIf=\"obs$ | async as value; else loading\"</code> work, and why is it a good pattern?","a":"<p>The <code>async</code> pipe subscribes to the observable and returns the latest value. The <code>as value</code> part saves it in a local variable, so the template subscribes once, and <code>value</code> can be used inside. While the value is falsy (for example before data arrives), the <code>else</code> branch shows the <code>&lt;ng-template #loading&gt;</code> placeholder. It also unsubscribes automatically on destroy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0047","card":"q-angular-013-structural-directives","sec":"angular-s015","q":"In <code>*ngFor</code>, why would you add <code>trackBy</code>, and what does it do?","a":"<p>Without <code>trackBy</code>, Angular identifies items by object identity, so when a new array of new objects arrives, it destroys and recreates every DOM node. With a <code>trackBy</code> function that returns a stable id, Angular reuses nodes for items with the same id, which saves DOM work and keeps state such as focus and input values.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0048","card":"q-angular-013-structural-directives","sec":"angular-s015","q":"What is <code>&lt;ng-template&gt;</code> and why does its content not appear on screen by itself?","a":"<p>It defines a block of markup that Angular does not render on its own. The content only appears when a directive, such as <code>*ngIf</code> with <code>else</code>, or code instantiates it. It is the building block behind structural directives.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0049","card":"q-angular-013-structural-directives","sec":"angular-s015","q":"How do you compose a page from several components?","a":"<p>Declare or import each component, then place it in the page template with its selector as an element, for example <code>&lt;app-user-list&gt;&lt;/app-user-list&gt;</code>. A module-based component needs the module that declares it to be imported. A standalone component must be listed in the <code>imports</code> of the component that uses it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0050","card":"q-angular-013-structural-directives","sec":"angular-s015","q":"In Angular 17 and later, what is the built-in control flow, and how does it compare with <code>*ngIf</code> and <code>*ngFor</code>?","a":"<p>Angular 17 added template blocks <code>@if</code>, <code>@for</code> and <code>@switch</code>, which replace the structural directives for new code. <code>@for</code> requires a <code>track</code> expression, and <code>@if</code> supports <code>@else</code>. They need no imports, have better type narrowing and are faster. <code>*ngIf</code> and <code>*ngFor</code> still work, but the CLI migration tool can convert them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0051","card":"q-angular-014-standalone-angular-14","sec":"angular-s016","q":"What is a standalone component, and what problem does it solve?","a":"<p>A standalone component, directive or pipe sets <code>standalone: true</code> and declares its own template dependencies in <code>imports</code>, so it needs no NgModule. This removes the boilerplate of modules and makes it clearer what a component uses. Angular 14 introduced it as a developer preview, and Angular 15 made it stable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0052","card":"q-angular-014-standalone-angular-14","sec":"angular-s016","q":"What is the default value of <code>standalone</code> in the decorator, and has it changed?","a":"<p>In Angular 14 to 18, the default is <code>false</code>, so you write <code>standalone: true</code>. Starting in Angular 19, components, directives and pipes are standalone by default, and a module-based one must say <code>standalone: false</code>. Check your project version before relying on the default.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0053","card":"q-angular-014-standalone-angular-14","sec":"angular-s016","q":"In a standalone component, why do you not inherit imports from a module, and what do you import instead?","a":"<p>Nothing is declared in a module, so there is no module scope to inherit. The component lists what its template uses in its own <code>imports</code>: other standalone components, directives and pipes, and modules such as <code>CommonModule</code> or <code>FormsModule</code>. If you forget one, the compiler reports an unknown element, pipe or property error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0054","card":"q-angular-014-standalone-angular-14","sec":"angular-s016","q":"What is the starting point of a standalone application, and how does it replace the root module?","a":"<p><code>bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, { providers: [...] })</code> in <code>main.ts</code>. App-wide providers such as the router and HTTP client are given with functions like <code>provideRouter(routes)</code> and <code>provideHttpClient()</code> in the config, replacing the root module's <code>imports</code> and <code>providers</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0055","card":"q-angular-014-standalone-angular-14","sec":"angular-s016","q":"How would you migrate a module-based application to standalone, and do you have to do it all at once?","a":"<p>No. Standalone and module code work together, so you migrate step by step. Start with leaf components, pipes and directives, set <code>standalone: true</code>, move them from <code>declarations</code> into <code>imports</code>, and update consumers. The CLI has a schematic, <code>ng generate @angular/core:standalone</code>, that converts code in stages. Keep modules where migration would be costly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0056","card":"q-angular-015-using-standalone-pieces-inside-a-module","sec":"angular-s017","q":"In a module-based Angular app, where do you register a standalone component, directive or pipe so that a module's components can use it, and why not in <code>declarations</code>?","a":"<p>In the <code>imports</code> of the NgModule that declares the consuming component, then use its selector as usual. A standalone class already owns its template context, and <code>declarations</code> is only for non-standalone classes, so declaring a standalone class there is a compiler error. Modules can import standalone pieces and standalone pieces can import modules, which allows gradual migration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0057","card":"q-angular-015-using-standalone-pieces-inside-a-module","sec":"angular-s017","q":"In a routing configuration, when do you use <code>loadComponent</code> and when <code>loadChildren</code>?","a":"<p>Use <code>loadComponent</code> to lazy load one standalone component. Use <code>loadChildren</code> to lazy load a module or a separate routes array (a feature with several child routes). Both use a dynamic <code>import()</code>, which makes a separate bundle that downloads on first navigation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0058","card":"q-angular-016-testing-a-standalone-component","sec":"angular-s018","q":"How do you configure <code>TestBed</code> for a standalone component?","a":"<p>Put the component in <code>imports</code>, not <code>declarations</code>, because a standalone class cannot be declared. Add providers for its dependencies, such as <code>provideHttpClient()</code> and <code>provideHttpClientTesting()</code>, then call <code>TestBed.createComponent(...)</code> and <code>fixture.detectChanges()</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0059","card":"q-angular-016-testing-a-standalone-component","sec":"angular-s018","q":"Why add <code>provideHttpClientTesting()</code> in a component test that calls an API service?","a":"<p>It replaces the real HTTP backend with a testing backend, so no real network request goes out. You can then control responses with <code>HttpTestingController</code>, check that the expected requests were made and test error cases. Without it, the component either would hit the network or fail on a missing provider.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0060","card":"q-angular-016-testing-a-standalone-component","sec":"angular-s018","q":"What does <code>fixture.detectChanges()</code> do in a component test, and why is it needed after <code>createComponent</code>?","a":"<p><code>TestBed.createComponent</code> builds the component, but it does not run change detection. <code>detectChanges()</code> runs it, which triggers <code>ngOnInit</code>, evaluates bindings and renders the template. Without it, the DOM stays empty and lifecycle code has not run. Call it again after changing state to update the view.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0061","card":"q-angular-017-zoneless-angular-18","sec":"angular-s019","q":"What is Zone.js in Angular, and what does it do for change detection?","a":"<p>Zone.js patches browser async APIs such as events, timers and promises. After any of these finish, Angular knows something may have changed and runs change detection. This is why a click handler updates the view with no extra code, at the cost of extra checks and bundle size.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0062","card":"q-angular-017-zoneless-angular-18","sec":"angular-s019","q":"What does zoneless Angular change, and what are the trade-offs?","a":"<p>Without Zone.js, Angular relies on explicit notifications, such as signal updates, the <code>async</code> pipe, <code>markForCheck</code> or events handled by the template, to schedule change detection. Benefits: fewer unnecessary checks, a smaller bundle, faster startup, clearer stack traces and better compatibility with libraries that do not work with Zone.js. Cost: code relying on implicit detection, such as mutable state changed in a <code>setTimeout</code>, will not update the view. It was experimental from Angular 18 and became stable in a later release, so check the current docs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0063","card":"q-angular-017-zoneless-angular-18","sec":"angular-s019","q":"Which coding practices make a component ready for zoneless operation?","a":"<p>Use signals for state that the template reads, use <code>OnPush</code> compatible components, use the <code>async</code> pipe for observables, and avoid mutating plain fields from async callbacks and expecting the view to update. These patterns notify Angular exactly when something changes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0064","card":"q-angular-018-7-pipes","sec":"angular-s020","q":"What is an Angular pipe, and when would you write one instead of a component method?","a":"<p>A pipe transforms a value inside a template, such as <code>{{ value | date }}</code>. Use a pipe for reusable display formatting. A pure pipe runs only when its input changes, while a method call in a template runs on every change detection pass, which is wasteful for non-trivial work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0065","card":"q-angular-018-7-pipes","sec":"angular-s020","q":"What is the syntax for pipe arguments and chaining in a template?","a":"<p>Arguments follow the pipe name, separated by colons: <code>{{ value | pipeName : arg1 : arg2 }}</code>. Chaining passes the output of one pipe into the next, from left to right: <code>{{ value | pipe1 | pipe2 }}</code>. Parentheses are needed when mixing a pipe with an operator like a ternary.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0066","card":"q-angular-018-7-pipes","sec":"angular-s020","q":"Why do built-in pipes need no import in a module-based app, but a custom or built-in pipe may need one in a standalone component?","a":"<p>Built-in pipes come from <code>CommonModule</code>, which module-based apps usually import. A standalone component has no module, so it must import <code>CommonModule</code> or the individual pipe class (for example <code>DatePipe</code>) in its own <code>imports</code>. Newer built-ins such as <code>AsyncPipe</code> and <code>DatePipe</code> are standalone and can be imported one by one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0067","card":"q-angular-019-built-in-pipes","sec":"angular-s021","q":"How would you show only the first five items of a list in a template?","a":"<p>Use the <code>slice</code> pipe in the <code>*ngFor</code> expression: <code>*ngFor=\"let item of items | slice : 0 : 5\"</code>. It works like JavaScript <code>slice</code>, with start index included and end index excluded, and does not change the original array.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0068","card":"q-angular-019-built-in-pipes","sec":"angular-s021","q":"What does the format string in the <code>percent</code> pipe, such as <code>'1.1-1'</code>, mean?","a":"<p>The format is <code>minIntegerDigits.minFractionDigits-maxFractionDigits</code>. For <code>'1.1-1'</code>, at least 1 integer digit, and exactly 1 fraction digit (min 1, max 1). So 0.5 shows as <code>50.0%</code>. With <code>'3.1-1'</code> it pads the integer part to <code>050.0%</code>. The pipe multiplies by 100 and uses the locale.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0069","card":"q-angular-019-built-in-pipes","sec":"angular-s021","q":"Which built-in pipes does Angular provide for text and data formatting?","a":"<p><code>uppercase</code>, <code>lowercase</code>, <code>titlecase</code>, <code>number</code>, <code>percent</code>, <code>currency</code>, <code>date</code>, <code>json</code>, <code>slice</code>, <code>keyvalue</code> and <code>async</code>. The number, percent, currency and date pipes use the locale to format values.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0070","card":"q-angular-019-built-in-pipes","sec":"angular-s021","q":"Why is <code>json</code> a useful pipe while debugging a template?","a":"<p><code>{{ obj | json }}</code> prints an object as formatted JSON, so you can check what data the template really receives, for example the shape of an HTTP response, without console logging. Remove it after debugging.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0071","card":"q-angular-020-custom-pipes","sec":"angular-s022","q":"What are the requirements for writing a custom pipe in Angular?","a":"<p>A class decorated with <code>@Pipe({ name: 'pipeName' })</code> that implements <code>PipeTransform</code> and defines <code>transform(value, ...args)</code>, which returns the new value. The <code>name</code> must be a valid identifier in camelCase, with no hyphens. The first argument is the value left of <code>|</code> and the rest are the pipe arguments. Register the pipe in a module's <code>declarations</code>, or make it <code>standalone: true</code> and import it where used.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0072","card":"q-angular-020-custom-pipes","sec":"angular-s022","q":"What is the difference between a pure and an impure pipe, and what is the default?","a":"<p>Pure (the default, <code>pure: true</code>) runs only when its input value or arguments change by reference, so it is fast. Impure (<code>pure: false</code>) runs on every change detection cycle, which is needed when it depends on mutable data or external state, but it can hurt performance. Prefer pure pipes with immutable data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0073","card":"q-angular-020-custom-pipes","sec":"angular-s022","q":"A pure pipe that sorts an array does not update when you push an item into the array. Why, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>A pure pipe is re-run only when the input reference changes, and <code>push</code> mutates the same array. Fix it by creating a new array, for example <code>this.items = [...this.items, item]</code>, or by using an impure pipe, which costs performance. Also note that <code>Array.sort</code> sorts in place, so a pipe that calls it on its input mutates the caller's data. Copy first with <code>[...value].sort(...)</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0074","card":"q-angular-020-custom-pipes","sec":"angular-s022","q":"In a custom pipe registered as <code>joinItems</code> whose method is <code>transform&lt;T extends unknown[]&gt;(value: T, separator = ', '): string</code>, what does the default parameter value do for the template?","a":"<p>The argument becomes optional, so <code>{{ list | joinItems }}</code> uses <code>', '</code> and <code>{{ list | joinItems : ' - ' }}</code> overrides it, assuming the pipe is registered under the name <code>joinItems</code>. Default parameter values are the way to give pipe arguments a sensible default.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0075","card":"q-angular-020-custom-pipes","sec":"angular-s022","q":"How do you test a custom pure pipe?","a":"<p>Create it with <code>new</code>, call <code>transform</code> with inputs and assert on the result. There is no need for <code>TestBed</code>, because a pipe with no injected dependencies is a plain class. You can test default arguments, custom arguments and edge cases such as an empty array.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0076","card":"q-angular-020-custom-pipes","sec":"angular-s022","q":"How do you make a custom pipe usable in several modules or standalone components?","a":"<p>For a module-based pipe, declare it in one module and add it to <code>exports</code>, then import that module where needed. For a standalone pipe (<code>standalone: true</code>), list the pipe class in the <code>imports</code> of each consumer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0077","card":"q-angular-021-8-services","sec":"angular-s023","q":"What is a service in Angular and why do you use one?","a":"<p>A service is a class that holds logic not tied to a view, such as API calls, shared state or calculations. It keeps components small and focused on the view, lets several components reuse the same logic and data, and is easy to replace in tests. Services are provided through dependency injection.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0078","card":"q-angular-021-8-services","sec":"angular-s023","q":"What does <code>@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })</code> do?","a":"<p>It registers the service with the root injector, so the whole app shares one singleton instance, and no <code>providers</code> entry is needed. It also avoids duplicated instances when a lazy module lists the service itself. Because it is tree-shakable, the service is dropped from the bundle if nothing uses it. It is the CLI default for new services.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0079","card":"q-angular-021-8-services","sec":"angular-s023","q":"When is a service a good place for shared state, and what are the risks of a singleton?","a":"<p>A singleton service lets unrelated components read and update the same data, for example the logged-in user or a cart. Risks are hidden coupling, state that lives for the whole app unless you reset it, and tests that affect each other if they share one instance. Expose state through signals or observables and keep updates in clearly named methods.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0080","card":"q-angular-022-canonical-http-service","sec":"angular-s024","q":"How do you make an HTTP request in Angular, and what does the generic type in <code>http.get&lt;User[]&gt;(url)</code> do?","a":"<p>Inject <code>HttpClient</code> and call a method such as <code>get</code>, <code>post</code>, <code>put</code> or <code>delete</code>. The generic type only informs TypeScript what the response body will look like, which gives autocomplete and compile-time checks. It does not validate the data at runtime.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0081","card":"q-angular-022-canonical-http-service","sec":"angular-s024","q":"What does <code>HttpClient.get</code> return, and when does the request actually happen?","a":"<p>It returns a cold Observable. The request is sent only when something subscribes (a <code>subscribe</code> call or the <code>async</code> pipe), and each subscription sends a new request. It completes after the response arrives.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0082","card":"q-angular-022-canonical-http-service","sec":"angular-s024","q":"Why should a service return an Observable from an HTTP call and let the component subscribe?","a":"<p>The component or the <code>async</code> pipe then controls when to start the request and when to cancel it. The service stays reusable, because callers can add operators such as <code>map</code>, <code>switchMap</code> or <code>catchError</code>, and tests can supply a fake observable. Subscribing inside the service hides this control from callers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0083","card":"q-angular-022-canonical-http-service","sec":"angular-s024","q":"How do you enable <code>HttpClient</code> in a module-based app, and in a standalone app?","a":"<p>Module-based: import <code>HttpClientModule</code> in a module, usually the root one. Standalone: add <code>provideHttpClient()</code> to the application providers. <code>HttpClientModule</code> is deprecated in Angular 18 and later in favor of <code>provideHttpClient()</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0084","card":"q-angular-022-canonical-http-service","sec":"angular-s024","q":"In an RxJS pipeline on an HTTP response, what is the difference between <code>map</code> and <code>delay</code>, and why might you use each in a service?","a":"<p><code>map</code> transforms each emitted value, for example picking one item out of a list response. <code>delay</code> postpones each emission by a time, which is used to simulate network latency in tests. Production services use <code>map</code> far more often than <code>delay</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0085","card":"q-angular-022-canonical-http-service","sec":"angular-s024","q":"What does the <code>$</code> suffix on a name such as <code>users$</code> mean?","a":"<p>It is a community naming convention for observables (Finnish notation). It has no effect on the code, but it tells the reader that the value must be subscribed to or used with the <code>async</code> pipe.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0086","card":"q-angular-022-canonical-http-service","sec":"angular-s024","q":"Why use <code>private</code> for constructor-injected dependencies?","a":"<p><code>private http: HttpClient</code> creates a class property that is limited to the class, so the template and other code cannot reach it, and unused dependencies are easy to spot. Use <code>public</code> only when the template needs to read it, and <code>protected</code> when subclasses need it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0087","card":"q-angular-023-other-service-shapes","sec":"angular-s025","q":"How would you build a service that exposes shared state with signals while preventing outside code from writing to it?","a":"<p>Keep a private writable signal and expose a read-only view:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">TypeScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>@Injectable({ providedIn: &#x27;root&#x27; })\nexport class StateService {\n  private state = signal&lt;State&gt;(initialState);\n  public readonly current = this.state.asReadonly();\n  public updateState(next: State) { this.state.set(next); }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Consumers can read <code>current()</code> but only the service can change the value through its methods.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0088","card":"q-angular-023-other-service-shapes","sec":"angular-s025","q":"In a service, how would you write a CRUD API wrapper with <code>HttpClient</code>?","a":"<p>Map each operation to an HTTP verb: <code>get</code> for read, <code>post</code> for create, <code>put</code> or <code>patch</code> for update, <code>delete</code> for delete. Each method returns the Observable, for example <code>return this.http.post&lt;T&gt;(url, data)</code>. Keep the base URL in one place, preferably from configuration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0089","card":"q-angular-023-other-service-shapes","sec":"angular-s025","q":"Why use <code>takeUntilDestroyed()</code> when subscribing inside a service or component?","a":"<p>It completes the subscription when the owning injection context is destroyed, which prevents leaks without writing <code>ngOnDestroy</code>. It comes from <code>@angular/core/rxjs-interop</code> (Angular 16+) and must be called in an injection context such as a constructor, or you pass a <code>DestroyRef</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0090","card":"q-angular-023-other-service-shapes","sec":"angular-s025","q":"Why would you call an HTTP request in a service constructor, and what is the drawback?","a":"<p>It starts loading data as soon as the service is created, so data is ready when components ask for it. The drawbacks are that the request is hidden in construction, harder to test and to retry, and starts even if nobody needs the data. Often an explicit <code>load()</code> method is clearer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0091","card":"q-angular-023-other-service-shapes","sec":"angular-s025","q":"Why does a small utility service such as an analytics tracker belong in a service and not in a component?","a":"<p>Many components need the same behavior, and putting it in one injectable class avoids duplicated code. Components stay free of storage or tracking details, and tests can replace it with a fake.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0092","card":"q-angular-024-injecting-services","sec":"angular-s026","q":"How do you inject a service into a component or another service?","a":"<p>Add it to the constructor with an access modifier, <code>constructor(private api: ApiService) {}</code>, and Angular provides the instance from the injector. In Angular 14 and later you can also call <code>inject(ApiService)</code> in a field initializer, which is useful in functions and with inheritance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0093","card":"q-angular-024-injecting-services","sec":"angular-s026","q":"What is the difference between constructor injection and the <code>inject()</code> function?","a":"<p>Both ask the injector for a token. Constructor injection is the classic style and is explicit in the constructor signature. <code>inject()</code> works in any injection context, such as field initializers, factory functions and functional guards, and avoids repeating constructor parameters in subclasses. Both give the same instance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0094","card":"q-angular-024-injecting-services","sec":"angular-s026","q":"A component injects a service that is not provided anywhere. What error do you get and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>Angular throws a <code>NullInjectorError: No provider for ...</code>. Fix it by adding <code>providedIn: 'root'</code> to the service decorator, or by adding the service to the <code>providers</code> of a module or component that is an ancestor of the injection point.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0095","card":"q-angular-025-9-directives","sec":"angular-s027","q":"What is an Angular directive, and what are the main kinds?","a":"<p>A directive adds behavior to an element or component in a template. There are three kinds: components (a directive with a template), attribute directives that change the look or behavior of an element, such as <code>ngClass</code>, and structural directives that add or remove elements, such as <code>*ngIf</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0096","card":"q-angular-025-9-directives","sec":"angular-s027","q":"When would you write a custom directive instead of a component?","a":"<p>Write a directive when the behavior applies to existing elements and needs no template of its own, for example highlighting on hover, trapping focus, or adding a tooltip. Write a component when you need a new piece of UI with its own markup.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0097","card":"q-angular-025-9-directives","sec":"angular-s027","q":"Why do custom attribute directives use a selector such as <code>[appHighlight]</code> with square brackets?","a":"<p>The brackets make it a CSS attribute selector, so the directive attaches to any element carrying that attribute. Without brackets, the selector would match an element with that tag name. The <code>app</code> prefix avoids clashes with standard attributes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0098","card":"q-angular-026-full-directive","sec":"angular-s028","q":"An attribute directive has the selector <code>[appStatus]</code> and declares <code>@Input() public appStatus: boolean | undefined;</code>. How does a template pass a value to it?","a":"<p>Bind it with the selector name on the same element: <code>&lt;li [appStatus]=\"user.online\"&gt;</code>. Because the input name matches the directive selector, the same attribute both attaches the directive and passes the value, with no separate input binding.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0099","card":"q-angular-026-full-directive","sec":"angular-s028","q":"In an Angular directive, what is the role of <code>ElementRef</code> and how do you get it?","a":"<p><code>ElementRef&lt;T&gt;</code> wraps the host element, and its <code>nativeElement</code> property is the actual DOM node. Angular injects it through the constructor: <code>constructor(private element: ElementRef&lt;HTMLAnchorElement&gt;) {}</code>. Use it to read or change the element the directive sits on.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0100","card":"q-angular-026-full-directive","sec":"angular-s028","q":"If one directive is applied to 20 list items, how many instances exist?","a":"<p>Twenty. Angular creates one directive instance per host element, each with its own <code>ElementRef</code> and input values.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0101","card":"q-angular-026-full-directive","sec":"angular-s028","q":"What is <code>@HostListener</code>, and when do you use it in a directive?","a":"<p>It subscribes a directive method to an event on the host element, for example <code>@HostListener('click') onClick() {...}</code>. Angular adds and removes the listener for you. It is cleaner than adding listeners by hand on <code>nativeElement</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0102","card":"q-angular-027-types-and-module-vs-standalone","sec":"angular-s029","q":"What is the difference between an attribute directive and a structural directive?","a":"<p>An attribute directive changes the appearance or behavior of an existing element, such as <code>ngClass</code> or a custom highlight. A structural directive changes the layout of the DOM by creating or removing views, it uses the <code>*</code> syntax, and it works with <code>TemplateRef</code> and <code>ViewContainerRef</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0103","card":"q-angular-027-types-and-module-vs-standalone","sec":"angular-s029","q":"How do you make a directive available in a template for a module-based app and for a standalone component?","a":"<p>Module-based: declare it in an NgModule's <code>declarations</code> and export it if other modules need it. Standalone: set <code>standalone: true</code> on the directive, and add the directive class to the <code>imports</code> of the consuming component or module. Forgetting this gives an unknown property error on the binding.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0104","card":"q-angular-028-elementref-vs-renderer2","sec":"angular-s030","q":"What is the difference between changing the DOM through <code>ElementRef.nativeElement</code> and through <code>Renderer2</code>?","a":"<p><code>nativeElement</code> gives the raw DOM node, so code works only where a real DOM exists, and writing HTML directly can open an XSS risk. <code>Renderer2</code> is an abstraction layer that Angular provides, so the same code works with server-side rendering and web workers, and its methods avoid unsafe HTML injection. Prefer <code>Renderer2</code> for writes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0105","card":"q-angular-028-elementref-vs-renderer2","sec":"angular-s030","q":"How would you set a background color on a directive's host element in a safe way?","a":"<p>Inject <code>ElementRef</code> and <code>Renderer2</code>, then call <code>this.renderer.setStyle(this.el.nativeElement, 'backgroundColor', 'yellow')</code>. An alternative with no DOM access is <code>@HostBinding('style.backgroundColor')</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0106","card":"q-angular-028-elementref-vs-renderer2","sec":"angular-s030","q":"Why is <code>element.nativeElement.innerHTML += value</code> risky?","a":"<p>It parses a string as HTML, which skips Angular's sanitization. If the value contains user input, it enables cross-site scripting. It also rebuilds the element's child nodes, which removes listeners and state. Create elements with <code>Renderer2.createElement</code> and <code>appendChild</code>, or use template bindings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0107","card":"q-angular-028-elementref-vs-renderer2","sec":"angular-s030","q":"How do you test a directive that injects <code>ElementRef</code>?","a":"<p>Provide a fake <code>ElementRef</code> in <code>TestBed</code>, such as a spy or an object with a <code>nativeElement</code>, and get the directive from the injector. For behavior tests, create a small host component that uses the directive in its template, then check the rendered DOM.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0108","card":"q-angular-029-tsconfig-json-project-root","sec":"angular-s031","q":"What is the difference between <code>compilerOptions</code> and <code>angularCompilerOptions</code> in <code>tsconfig.json</code>?","a":"<p><code>compilerOptions</code> are standard TypeScript settings, such as <code>strict</code>, <code>target</code> and <code>module</code>. <code>angularCompilerOptions</code> configure the Angular compiler, such as <code>strictTemplates</code> and <code>strictInjectionParameters</code>. Both live in the same file.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0109","card":"q-angular-029-tsconfig-json-project-root","sec":"angular-s031","q":"Why does an Angular project need <code>experimentalDecorators</code> in <code>tsconfig.json</code>?","a":"<p>Angular's decorators (<code>@Component</code>, <code>@Injectable</code> and so on) use the TypeScript decorator syntax, and TypeScript requires this flag to compile it. Without it, the compiler reports an error on every decorator.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0110","card":"q-angular-029-tsconfig-json-project-root","sec":"angular-s031","q":"What does <code>strictTemplates</code> do, and why enable it?","a":"<p>It makes the Angular compiler type-check template bindings with the same strictness as TypeScript code. It catches mistakes like passing a string to a number input or reading a property that might be undefined, at build time and not in the browser. The cost is more errors to fix in older code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0111","card":"q-angular-029-tsconfig-json-project-root","sec":"angular-s031","q":"Why does an Angular project have <code>tsconfig.app.json</code> and <code>tsconfig.spec.json</code> next to <code>tsconfig.json</code>?","a":"<p>They extend the base file with settings specific to a build: the app config includes the application files, and the spec config includes test files and adds the testing types, for example Jasmine. This keeps test-only types out of the production build.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0112","card":"q-angular-029-tsconfig-json-project-root","sec":"angular-s031","q":"What does <code>strict: true</code> enable in TypeScript, and why is it recommended in Angular projects?","a":"<p>It turns on a group of checks, including <code>strictNullChecks</code>, <code>noImplicitAny</code> and strict function types. It catches null and type errors at compile time. New Angular projects enable it by default.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0113","card":"q-angular-030-environments","sec":"angular-s032","q":"What problem do Angular environment files solve?","a":"<p>They let one codebase use different configuration per build, such as API URLs, feature flags or log levels, without <code>if</code> statements. In the Angular 15+ layout, new projects have no environment files by default and you add them with <code>ng generate environments</code>, which creates <code>src/environments/environment.ts</code> (the production values) and <code>environment.development.ts</code>. Before Angular 15, new projects came with <code>environment.ts</code> and <code>environment.prod.ts</code>. In both layouts the build swaps in the right file.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0114","card":"q-angular-030-environments","sec":"angular-s032","q":"How does the Angular build pick the right environment file, and which file should code import?","a":"<p>Code always imports the base file, <code>environment.ts</code>. In the Angular 15+ layout, the development configuration in <code>angular.json</code> has <code>fileReplacements</code> that swap it for <code>environment.development.ts</code> (older projects swapped in <code>environment.prod.ts</code> for production instead). This means the import path never changes and no code branches on the environment.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0115","card":"q-angular-030-environments","sec":"angular-s032","q":"Why must all environment files export the same properties with the same types?","a":"<p>Code is compiled against the base file's shape, and the build swaps in another file. If a property is missing from one, it would be <code>undefined</code> at runtime in that build, though the compiler checked against another file. Keeping the same shape prevents this.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0116","card":"q-angular-030-environments","sec":"angular-s032","q":"Is it safe to put API secrets in an Angular environment file?","a":"<p>No. Environment values are compiled into the JavaScript bundle that every user downloads, so anything there is public. Put only non-secret configuration such as base URLs and flags. Keep secrets on a backend that calls third-party services on behalf of the client.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0117","card":"q-angular-030-environments","sec":"angular-s032","q":"What is the difference between environment files and runtime configuration?","a":"<p>Environment files are fixed at build time, so one build per environment is needed. Runtime configuration loads a JSON file or an endpoint when the app starts, for example through an app initializer, so one build artifact can run in many environments. Choose runtime configuration when you want to build once and deploy everywhere.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0118","card":"q-angular-031-11-dependency-injection","sec":"angular-s033","q":"What is dependency injection, and what benefits does it give?","a":"<p>Dependency injection is a pattern where a class asks for what it needs and an external system supplies it, instead of creating it. It reduces coupling, allows sharing of instances, and lets tests replace real dependencies with fakes. Angular has a built-in DI system, and components, directives, pipes and services all use it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0119","card":"q-angular-031-11-dependency-injection","sec":"angular-s033","q":"What are the three roles in Angular's DI system?","a":"<p>A consumer (a class that needs a dependency), a token (the key that identifies it, often the class itself), and a provider (the recipe that says how to create the value). The injector looks up the token, finds the provider, creates the value once per injector and returns it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0120","card":"q-angular-031-11-dependency-injection","sec":"angular-s033","q":"What is the injector hierarchy in Angular, and how does a lookup proceed?","a":"<p>Injectors form a tree: the element injector tree follows the component tree, and module or environment injectors sit higher up, ending at the root. A lookup starts at the consuming component's injector and moves up until a provider is found. If none is found, you get a <code>NullInjectorError</code>. This is why a component-level provider overrides a root one for that subtree.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0121","card":"q-angular-032-how-it-works","sec":"angular-s034","q":"In <code>constructor(private api: ApiService)</code>, how does Angular know what to inject?","a":"<p>The parameter's type annotation is used as the token. Angular reads this metadata at compile time, looks the token up in the injector and passes the matching instance. For non-class tokens, such as strings or other values, you create an <code>InjectionToken</code> (or string token) and use it with <code>@Inject(TOKEN)</code> or <code>inject(TOKEN)</code>. Interfaces cannot be tokens at all, because they do not exist at runtime.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0122","card":"q-angular-032-how-it-works","sec":"angular-s034","q":"Why can you not use a TypeScript interface as a DI token?","a":"<p>Interfaces are erased when TypeScript compiles to JavaScript, so there is nothing at runtime to use as a key. Use a class, a string, or an <code>InjectionToken</code> as the token, and keep the interface for the value's type.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0123","card":"q-angular-032-how-it-works","sec":"angular-s034","q":"What kinds of values can Angular's injector supply besides services?","a":"<p>Anything that has a provider: configuration objects, strings, numbers, booleans, functions, and factory results. You register them with a token and one of <code>useValue</code>, <code>useClass</code>, <code>useFactory</code> or <code>useExisting</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0124","card":"q-angular-033-constructor-injection-syntax","sec":"angular-s035","q":"What is the difference between <code>constructor(private api: ApiService)</code> and <code>constructor(api: ApiService)</code>?","a":"<p>With an access modifier (<code>private</code>, <code>public</code>, <code>protected</code>, <code>readonly</code>), TypeScript declares a class property and assigns the argument to it. Without a modifier, <code>api</code> is only a local parameter in the constructor and is not available in other methods.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0125","card":"q-angular-033-constructor-injection-syntax","sec":"angular-s035","q":"When does an injected service need to be <code>public</code> in a component?","a":"<p>When the template reads it directly, such as <code>{{ cartService.total() }}</code>, because templates can only access public members. If only the class code uses it, keep it <code>private</code>. Under AOT compilation, a private member used in a template is a compile error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0126","card":"q-angular-034-declaring-dependencies-injectable-and-pr","sec":"angular-s036","q":"What are the options for <code>providedIn</code>, and what does each mean?","a":"<p><code>'root'</code> creates one app-wide singleton and is tree-shakable. Leaving it out (a bare <code>@Injectable()</code>) means you must add the class to a <code>providers</code> array, which gives control over the scope. <code>'platform'</code> shares a single instance across all Angular applications on the page, which is rare.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0127","card":"q-angular-035-providing-dependencies","sec":"angular-s037","q":"What is the shorthand <code>providers: [ApiService]</code> equivalent to?","a":"<p><code>providers: [{ provide: ApiService, useClass: ApiService }]</code>. <code>provide</code> is the token, and <code>useClass</code> tells the injector which class to instantiate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0128","card":"q-angular-035-providing-dependencies","sec":"angular-s037","q":"When would you use <code>useClass</code> with a different class, such as <code>{ provide: ApiService, useClass: MockApiService }</code>?","a":"<p>To swap an implementation without changing consumers: a fake service in tests, a mock backend in development, or a different strategy per environment. The consumer still asks for <code>ApiService</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0129","card":"q-angular-035-providing-dependencies","sec":"angular-s037","q":"What does <code>useValue</code> do, and what is a typical use?","a":"<p>It supplies a ready-made value, with no construction. Typical uses are configuration objects, constants, feature flags and test doubles, for example <code>{ provide: API_CONFIG, useValue: { url: '...', timeout: 5000 } }</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0130","card":"q-angular-035-providing-dependencies","sec":"angular-s037","q":"When do you use <code>useFactory</code>, and what is <code>deps</code> for?","a":"<p>Use <code>useFactory</code> when the value must be computed or needs other dependencies, for example choosing a logger by environment or building a client from <code>HttpClient</code>. <code>deps</code> lists the tokens whose instances are passed to the factory function in order, because a plain function cannot use constructor injection.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0131","card":"q-angular-035-providing-dependencies","sec":"angular-s037","q":"What does <code>useExisting</code> do, and how is it different from <code>useClass</code>?","a":"<p>It makes one token an alias for another, so both resolve to the same instance. <code>useClass</code> creates a new instance for the token. Use <code>useExisting</code> to keep an old token working while moving to a new class.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0132","card":"q-angular-035-providing-dependencies","sec":"angular-s037","q":"Why prefer an <code>InjectionToken</code> over a string token?","a":"<p><code>new InjectionToken&lt;ApiConfig&gt;('api.config')</code> is unique, so two libraries cannot collide on the same name, and it carries the type, so <code>inject(API_CONFIG)</code> is typed. A string token has neither, and a typo fails at runtime.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0133","card":"q-angular-035-providing-dependencies","sec":"angular-s037","q":"How do you inject a non-class value such as a base URL into a service?","a":"<p>Define an <code>InjectionToken&lt;string&gt;</code>, provide it with <code>{ provide: API_URL, useValue: 'https://api.example.com' }</code>, and inject it with <code>@Inject(API_URL)</code> in the constructor or with <code>inject(API_URL)</code>. A plain string type annotation cannot work as a token.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0134","card":"q-angular-035-providing-dependencies","sec":"angular-s037","q":"How does providing a service at component level change its lifetime?","a":"<p>A new instance is created for each instance of the component and is destroyed with it. Child components receive the same instance, but sibling components do not. This suits state that belongs to one screen, such as a form wizard.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0135","card":"q-angular-036-change-detection","sec":"angular-s038","q":"What is change detection in Angular, and when does it run?","a":"<p>It is the process that compares the current state with what is on screen and updates the DOM. By default it runs after events, timers, promise resolution and HTTP responses, because Zone.js notifies Angular after each. It walks the component tree from the root.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0136","card":"q-angular-036-change-detection","sec":"angular-s038","q":"What is the difference between the <code>Default</code> and <code>OnPush</code> change detection strategies?","a":"<p><code>Default</code> checks every component in the tree on each pass. <code>OnPush</code> checks a component only when an input reference changes, an event originates in it or its children, an <code>async</code> pipe or signal it reads emits, or detection is triggered manually. <code>OnPush</code> reduces work and is the usual choice for performance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0137","card":"q-angular-036-change-detection","sec":"angular-s038","q":"A component with <code>OnPush</code> does not update after you push an item into an input array. Why, and what do you do?","a":"<p><code>OnPush</code> compares the input by reference, and <code>push</code> keeps the same reference, so Angular sees no change. Replace the array with a new one (<code>items = [...items, item]</code>) or use immutable updates. Alternatively use signals or call <code>markForCheck()</code>, which is a last resort.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0138","card":"q-angular-036-change-detection","sec":"angular-s038","q":"Why do <code>OnPush</code> and the <code>async</code> pipe work well together?","a":"<p>Each emission from the observable is a new value reference, and the <code>async</code> pipe marks the component for checking when it emits. So the view updates without manual calls, and the component is skipped when nothing changes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0139","card":"q-angular-036-change-detection","sec":"angular-s038","q":"What does <code>ChangeDetectorRef.markForCheck()</code> do, and when would you use it?","a":"<p>It marks the component and its ancestors to be checked on the next change detection pass, even under <code>OnPush</code>. Use it when state changes outside the usual triggers, such as a non-template subscription. Prefer the <code>async</code> pipe or signals, which do this for you.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0140","card":"q-angular-036-change-detection","sec":"angular-s038","q":"How do signals change the way Angular decides what to update?","a":"<p>A template that reads a signal is registered as a consumer of it. When the signal changes, Angular knows which view must be refreshed, so it can update that part without checking the whole tree. Signals are available since Angular 16 and are stable in later versions, so check your version.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0141","card":"q-angular-037-lifecycle-hooks","sec":"angular-s039","q":"In which order do the Angular lifecycle hooks run when a component is created?","a":"<p>Constructor, <code>ngOnChanges</code> (only if there are inputs), <code>ngOnInit</code>, <code>ngDoCheck</code>, <code>ngAfterContentInit</code>, <code>ngAfterContentChecked</code>, <code>ngAfterViewInit</code>, <code>ngAfterViewChecked</code>. On later updates: <code>ngOnChanges</code>, <code>ngDoCheck</code>, <code>ngAfterContentChecked</code>, <code>ngAfterViewChecked</code>. At the end: <code>ngOnDestroy</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0142","card":"q-angular-037-lifecycle-hooks","sec":"angular-s039","q":"Why use <code>ngOnInit</code> for initialization and not the constructor?","a":"<p>The constructor runs when the class is instantiated, before inputs are bound, and should only receive dependencies. <code>ngOnInit</code> runs once after the first inputs are set, so you can use them, and it is the place for data loading. It also keeps the class easy to construct in tests.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0143","card":"q-angular-037-lifecycle-hooks","sec":"angular-s039","q":"What does <code>ngOnChanges</code> receive, and when does it fire?","a":"<p>It receives a <code>SimpleChanges</code> object that maps each changed input name to its previous and current value. It fires before <code>ngOnInit</code> and whenever a bound input reference changes. It does not fire when you mutate an object in place, or for changes made inside the component.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0144","card":"q-angular-037-lifecycle-hooks","sec":"angular-s039","q":"What belongs in <code>ngOnDestroy</code>, and why?","a":"<p>Cleanup that Angular does not do for you: unsubscribing from observables, clearing timers and intervals, and removing manual event listeners. Skipping it leaks memory and may keep callbacks running after the component is gone.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0145","card":"q-angular-037-lifecycle-hooks","sec":"angular-s039","q":"What is the difference between <code>ngAfterContentInit</code> and <code>ngAfterViewInit</code>?","a":"<p><code>ngAfterContentInit</code> runs after content projected into the component with <code>&lt;ng-content&gt;</code> is initialized. <code>ngAfterViewInit</code> runs after the component's own template and its child views are initialized, and it is the first place <code>@ViewChild</code> results are available.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0146","card":"q-angular-037-lifecycle-hooks","sec":"angular-s039","q":"Why should you implement the hook interfaces, such as <code>implements OnInit</code>?","a":"<p>The interface makes the compiler check that the method exists with the right name and signature. A typo like <code>ngOnInt</code> would otherwise silently never run. It also documents which hooks the class uses.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0147","card":"q-angular-037-lifecycle-hooks","sec":"angular-s039","q":"What is <code>ngDoCheck</code> for, and what is the risk?","a":"<p>It runs on every change detection pass, so you can add custom checks, for example detecting in-place changes that Angular does not see. Heavy work there slows the whole app, so keep it very light.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0148","card":"q-angular-037-lifecycle-hooks","sec":"angular-s039","q":"In a component that subscribes in <code>ngOnInit</code>, how do you clean up with a field and <code>ngOnDestroy</code>?","a":"<p>Store the <code>Subscription</code> in a field, then call <code>unsubscribe()</code> in <code>ngOnDestroy</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">TypeScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>private subscription?: Subscription;\n\nngOnInit(): void {\n  this.subscription = this.api.getData$().subscribe();\n}\n\nngOnDestroy(): void {\n  this.subscription?.unsubscribe();\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>The modern alternatives are the <code>async</code> pipe or <code>takeUntilDestroyed()</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0149","card":"q-angular-038-13-routing-and-navigation","sec":"angular-s040","q":"What does the Angular Router do, and how is it set up?","a":"<p>It maps URLs to components, renders the matching component in a <code>&lt;router-outlet&gt;</code>, and updates the browser history without a page reload. You define a <code>Routes</code> array and register it with <code>RouterModule.forRoot(routes)</code> in a module-based app or <code>provideRouter(routes)</code> in a standalone app. The router is a separate package, <code>@angular/router</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0150","card":"q-angular-038-13-routing-and-navigation","sec":"angular-s040","q":"Why does route order matter in a <code>Routes</code> array?","a":"<p>The router uses the first route that matches, in array order. Put specific routes first, then the empty-path redirect, then the <code>**</code> wildcard last. If the wildcard comes earlier, it matches everything, and later routes are never reached.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0151","card":"q-angular-038-13-routing-and-navigation","sec":"angular-s040","q":"What does <code>&lt;base href=\"/\"&gt;</code> in <code>index.html</code> do for the router?","a":"<p>It sets the root URL that relative paths are resolved against. The router builds links relative to it, so an app served from a sub-path must set it to that path, or use the build option <code>--base-href</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0152","card":"q-angular-038-13-routing-and-navigation","sec":"angular-s040","q":"How do you handle an unknown URL in Angular routing?","a":"<p>Add a wildcard route <code>{ path: '**', ... }</code> as the last entry. It can redirect to a known route or show a \"not found\" component. It must be last because it matches any URL.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0153","card":"q-angular-039-defining-routes","sec":"angular-s041","q":"Why does the redirect route <code>{ path: '', redirectTo: '/users', pathMatch: 'full' }</code> need <code>pathMatch: 'full'</code>?","a":"<p>The default <code>pathMatch</code> is <code>'prefix'</code>, and an empty path is a prefix of every URL, so the redirect would match every URL and loop or hijack all navigation. With <code>'full'</code>, the route matches only when the whole remaining URL is empty.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0154","card":"q-angular-039-defining-routes","sec":"angular-s041","q":"In a routes array that sets <code>title: (route: ActivatedRouteSnapshot) =&gt; ...</code>, what does a title function enable?","a":"<p>It builds the page title from the route, such as its parameters or resolved data, and Angular sets the document title after navigation. A plain string works for static titles. The <code>title</code> option exists since Angular 14.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0155","card":"q-angular-039-defining-routes","sec":"angular-s041","q":"What is the difference between <code>redirectTo: '/users'</code> and <code>redirectTo: 'users'</code>?","a":"<p>A leading slash makes it an absolute redirect from the root of the app. Without it, the redirect is relative to the parent route's path. In nested routes this difference decides where the user ends up.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0156","card":"q-angular-039-defining-routes","sec":"angular-s041","q":"What does <code>RouterModule.forRoot</code> do compared to <code>forChild</code>, and why call <code>forRoot</code> only once?","a":"<p><code>forRoot</code> registers the router service itself plus the root routes, so it should be called only in the root module. <code>forChild</code> only adds route definitions for a feature module and does not create another router. Calling <code>forRoot</code> more than once would create conflicting router instances.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0157","card":"q-angular-039-defining-routes","sec":"angular-s041","q":"What does <code>useHash: true</code> in the router options do?","a":"<p>It uses URLs of the form <code>/#/users</code>, so the server only ever sees the root path. It helps when you cannot configure the server to return <code>index.html</code> for deep links. The default path-based URLs look cleaner and are preferred when the server supports it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0158","card":"q-angular-039-defining-routes","sec":"angular-s041","q":"Why does a deep link to <code>/users</code> return a 404 on a plain static server but work in <code>ng serve</code>, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>The route exists only in the browser. A static server looks for a file at that path and finds none. Configure the server to return <code>index.html</code> for unknown paths (a rewrite or fallback rule), or use hash routing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0159","card":"q-angular-040-route-properties","sec":"angular-s042","q":"What do <code>path</code>, <code>component</code>, <code>redirectTo</code>, <code>pathMatch</code>, <code>data</code> and <code>title</code> mean in a route object?","a":"<p><code>path</code> is the URL segment to match. <code>component</code> is what to render. <code>redirectTo</code> sends the user to another path. <code>pathMatch</code> sets prefix or full matching. <code>data</code> is a static object of custom metadata, such as roles or flags, that you read from <code>ActivatedRoute</code>. <code>title</code> sets the page title.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0160","card":"q-angular-040-route-properties","sec":"angular-s042","q":"How would you attach custom metadata to a route and read it in a component?","a":"<p>Add <code>data: { role: 'admin' }</code> to the route. In the component, inject <code>ActivatedRoute</code> and read <code>this.route.snapshot.data['role']</code>, or subscribe to <code>this.route.data</code>. Guards and preloading strategies can also read it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0161","card":"q-angular-041-lazy-loading","sec":"angular-s043","q":"What is lazy loading in Angular routing, and what is its main benefit?","a":"<p>The code of a route is split into a separate bundle and downloaded only when the user first navigates there. The initial bundle is smaller, so the first load is faster. The cost is a short delay on first navigation to that route, noticeable on slow networks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0162","card":"q-angular-041-lazy-loading","sec":"angular-s043","q":"Explain <code>() =&gt; import('./users/users.module').then(m =&gt; m.UsersModule)</code> in a route.","a":"<p><code>import()</code> is a dynamic ES module import that returns a Promise and tells the bundler to create a separate chunk. When the promise resolves, <code>.then</code> extracts the exported module class from the loaded file. The arrow function delays all this until the route is first needed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0163","card":"q-angular-041-lazy-loading","sec":"angular-s043","q":"Which things prevent a lazy-loaded module from actually being lazy?","a":"<p>Importing that module, or any of its components, statically from the root module or another eagerly loaded file. The bundler then includes it in the main bundle. A lazy feature should be referenced only through the route's dynamic import.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0164","card":"q-angular-041-lazy-loading","sec":"angular-s043","q":"When should you not lazy load a route?","a":"<p>For the landing page or routes nearly every user hits at once, since lazy loading would add a request without shrinking the work the user needs. Very small features may not be worth the extra chunk. Preloading can reduce the delay for the rest.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0165","card":"q-angular-042-preloading-strategies","sec":"angular-s044","q":"What is preloading in the Angular router, and what problem does it address?","a":"<p>Preloading downloads lazy routes in the background after the initial load, so later navigation is instant while the first load stays small. It resolves the main drawback of lazy loading, which is a delay on first visit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0166","card":"q-angular-042-preloading-strategies","sec":"angular-s044","q":"What are the built-in preloading strategies, and how do they differ?","a":"<p><code>NoPreloading</code> is the default and loads lazy routes only on demand. <code>PreloadAllModules</code> loads every lazy route in the background after startup, which is fine for small apps but wastes bandwidth with many routes. You can also write a custom strategy that picks routes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0167","card":"q-angular-042-preloading-strategies","sec":"angular-s044","q":"How would you preload only selected routes?","a":"<p>Write a class that implements <code>PreloadingStrategy</code> with a <code>preload(route, load)</code> method that returns <code>load()</code> for routes that should preload and <code>of(null)</code> for the others. Mark routes with <code>data: { preload: true }</code>, check that flag, and register it with <code>RouterModule.forRoot(routes, { preloadingStrategy: MyStrategy })</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0168","card":"q-angular-042-preloading-strategies","sec":"angular-s044","q":"In a custom <code>PreloadingStrategy</code>, why does the flag need bracket access, as in <code>route.data['preload']</code>?","a":"<p><code>Route.data</code> is typed as an index signature (<code>Data</code>), and TypeScript with <code>noPropertyAccessFromIndexSignature</code> requires bracket access for such keys. Dot notation would give a compile error under that setting.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0169","card":"q-angular-043-dynamic-route-parameters","sec":"angular-s045","q":"How do you define and read a route parameter such as <code>/profile/123</code>?","a":"<p>Define the route with a placeholder, <code>path: 'profile/:id'</code>. In the component, inject <code>ActivatedRoute</code> and read <code>this.route.snapshot.paramMap.get('id')</code>, or subscribe to <code>this.route.paramMap</code>. With <code>withComponentInputBinding()</code> in the router config (Angular 16+), the parameter can be bound directly to a component <code>@Input</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0170","card":"q-angular-043-dynamic-route-parameters","sec":"angular-s045","q":"What is the difference between <code>ActivatedRoute</code> and <code>ActivatedRouteSnapshot</code>?","a":"<p><code>ActivatedRouteSnapshot</code> is a fixed view of the route at one moment, such as when navigation completes, and is handy in functions like a <code>title</code> resolver or guards. <code>ActivatedRoute</code> is injected into components and exposes observables such as <code>paramMap</code>, which emit when values change while the component stays alive.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0171","card":"q-angular-043-dynamic-route-parameters","sec":"angular-s045","q":"Why does reading <code>snapshot.paramMap</code> give stale data when navigating from <code>/profile/1</code> to <code>/profile/2</code>?","a":"<p>Angular reuses the same component instance when only the parameter changes, so <code>ngOnInit</code> does not run again, and the snapshot taken at creation stays at the old value. Subscribe to <code>paramMap</code> instead, which emits the new parameter.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0172","card":"q-angular-043-dynamic-route-parameters","sec":"angular-s045","q":"Why is <code>switchMap</code> used on top of <code>paramMap</code> to load data?","a":"<p><code>switchMap</code> starts a new inner request for each new parameter and cancels the previous one if it is still running. This prevents an older, slower response from overwriting a newer one, so the view always shows the data for the latest parameter.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0173","card":"q-angular-043-dynamic-route-parameters","sec":"angular-s045","q":"What is the difference between route parameters, query parameters and the fragment?","a":"<p>Route parameters are part of the path (<code>/profile/123</code>) and identify a resource, read with <code>paramMap</code>. Query parameters follow <code>?</code> (<code>/users?search=john</code>) and are optional filters, read with <code>queryParamMap</code>. The fragment follows <code>#</code> and points to a place on the page, read with <code>fragment</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0174","card":"q-angular-043-dynamic-route-parameters","sec":"angular-s045","q":"With <code>ParamMap</code>, when would you use <code>get</code>, <code>getAll</code> and <code>has</code>?","a":"<p><code>get('id')</code> returns the first value or <code>null</code>. <code>getAll('tag')</code> returns an array for repeated parameters. <code>has('id')</code> checks whether the parameter exists. <code>keys</code> lists all names.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0175","card":"q-angular-044-router-in-templates","sec":"angular-s046","q":"What is <code>&lt;router-outlet&gt;</code> and where does the routed component appear?","a":"<p>It is a placeholder component where the router renders the component for the active route. The routed component is inserted as a sibling directly after the outlet element, not inside it. Nested routes need an outlet in the parent's template.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0176","card":"q-angular-044-router-in-templates","sec":"angular-s046","q":"Why use <code>routerLink</code> instead of <code>href</code> for navigation inside the app?","a":"<p>A normal <code>href</code> triggers a full page reload, losing app state. <code>routerLink</code> navigates through the router, which only swaps components and updates the URL and history. It also builds the correct URL for the current base href and route.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0177","card":"q-angular-044-router-in-templates","sec":"angular-s046","q":"How do you highlight the current menu link in an Angular nav?","a":"<p>Add <code>routerLinkActive=\"active\"</code> to the link. The router adds that CSS class when the link's route is active, and removes it otherwise. Then style the class, for example <code>.nav-link.active</code>. Add <code>[routerLinkActiveOptions]=\"{ exact: true }\"</code> when a parent path would match too many links.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0178","card":"q-angular-044-router-in-templates","sec":"angular-s046","q":"What is the difference between <code>routerLink=\"users\"</code>, <code>routerLink=\"/users\"</code> and <code>[routerLink]=\"['/profile', id]\"</code>?","a":"<p><code>users</code> is relative to the current route. <code>/users</code> is absolute from the root. The array form builds a path from segments, so each element becomes one segment, and it allows dynamic values such as an id. Square brackets are needed for dynamic values.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0179","card":"q-angular-044-router-in-templates","sec":"angular-s046","q":"How do you navigate from code instead of a link?","a":"<p>Inject <code>Router</code> and call <code>this.router.navigate(['/profile', id])</code> or <code>navigateByUrl('/users')</code>. Use it after an action, such as saving a form. Both return a Promise of whether navigation succeeded.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0180","card":"q-angular-045-signals-angular-16-developer-preview-at","sec":"angular-s047","q":"What is a signal in Angular, and how do you read and write it?","a":"<p>A signal is a reactive wrapper around a value that notifies its consumers when it changes. Create it with <code>signal(initial)</code>, read it by calling it (<code>count()</code>), and write with <code>set(value)</code> or <code>update(fn)</code>. Signals were introduced in Angular 16 as a developer preview and became stable in later versions (core signal APIs in Angular 17), so check your version.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0181","card":"q-angular-045-signals-angular-16-developer-preview-at","sec":"angular-s047","q":"What is the difference between <code>set</code> and <code>update</code> on a writable signal?","a":"<p><code>set(value)</code> replaces the value with a new one. <code>update(fn)</code> receives the current value and returns the next one, for example <code>count.update(n =&gt; n + 1)</code>. Use <code>update</code> when the new value depends on the old one. Both should produce a new value and not mutate the old one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0182","card":"q-angular-045-signals-angular-16-developer-preview-at","sec":"angular-s047","q":"What happened to <code>signal.mutate()</code>?","a":"<p>It changed a value in place and was available in the Angular 16 preview. It was removed in Angular 17, because in-place changes made equality checks and dependency tracking harder to reason about. Use <code>update</code> with a new array or object, for example <code>items.update(list =&gt; [...list, item])</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0183","card":"q-angular-045-signals-angular-16-developer-preview-at","sec":"angular-s047","q":"What is a <code>computed</code> signal, and why is it better than a method that derives the value?","a":"<p><code>computed(() =&gt; this.items().length)</code> derives a read-only value from other signals. It is lazy, so it runs only when read, and cached, so it recalculates only when a signal it depends on changes. A method in a template runs on every change detection pass. A computed signal cannot be set directly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0184","card":"q-angular-045-signals-angular-16-developer-preview-at","sec":"angular-s047","q":"What error do you get if you call <code>set</code> on a computed signal, and why?","a":"<p>A compile-time error, because the <code>set</code> method does not exist on a read-only computed signal. Its value is always derived, so changing it directly would break the relationship with the signals it depends on.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0185","card":"q-angular-045-signals-angular-16-developer-preview-at","sec":"angular-s047","q":"How do signals compare with RxJS observables?","a":"<p>Signals are synchronous and always hold a current value, with a simple API, which suits local UI state. Observables are streams of values over time with rich operators for cancellation, debouncing, combining and async work, which suits HTTP and events. They work together through <code>toSignal</code> and <code>toObservable</code> in <code>@angular/core/rxjs-interop</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0186","card":"q-angular-046-signals-angular-16-developer-preview-at","sec":"angular-s047","q":"How would you build a filtered list with signals?","a":"<p>Keep the source and the search term as signals and derive the result with <code>computed</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">TypeScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>public items = signal&lt;Item[]&gt;([]);\npublic searchTerm = signal(&#x27;&#x27;);\npublic filteredItems = computed(() =&gt; {\n  const term = this.searchTerm().toLowerCase();\n  return this.items().filter(item =&gt; item.name.toLowerCase().includes(term));\n});</code></pre></div>\n<p>The list updates when either signal changes, and the work is cached otherwise.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0187","card":"q-angular-046-signals-angular-16-developer-preview-at","sec":"angular-s047","q":"How do you add and remove items in a list held in a signal?","a":"<p>Use <code>update</code> with new arrays: <code>this.items.update(list =&gt; [...list, item])</code> to add and <code>this.items.update(list =&gt; list.filter(i =&gt; i.id !== id))</code> to remove. Creating a new array keeps updates immutable, which works with <code>OnPush</code> and equality checks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0188","card":"q-angular-046-signals-angular-16-developer-preview-at","sec":"angular-s047","q":"Why do dependencies in a <code>computed</code> need to be read by calling the signal inside the callback?","a":"<p>Angular tracks which signals are read while the callback runs, and those become the dependencies. A signal not read in the callback (or read outside it) will not trigger recalculation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0189","card":"q-angular-046-signals-angular-16-developer-preview-at","sec":"angular-s047","q":"How would you validate a form-like state using signals?","a":"<p>Hold each field in a signal and derive validity with <code>computed</code>, for example <code>isValid = computed(() =&gt; this.username().length &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; this.email().includes('@'))</code>. The template reads <code>isValid()</code> and updates whenever a field changes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0190","card":"q-angular-047-effect","sec":"angular-s048","q":"What is an <code>effect()</code> in Angular, and when do you use it?","a":"<p>An effect runs a function whenever the signals it reads change. Use it for side effects that leave the signal graph: logging, analytics, syncing to <code>localStorage</code>, or updating the document title. Do not use it to derive state, because <code>computed</code> is made for that.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0191","card":"q-angular-047-effect","sec":"angular-s048","q":"Why should you not use <code>effect</code> to copy one signal into another?","a":"<p>It adds an extra run, can cause ordering problems and hides the data flow. Use <code>computed</code> for derived values or <code>linkedSignal</code> (newer versions). Writing signals inside effects is restricted by default in Angular 16 to 18 and discouraged in all versions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0192","card":"q-angular-047-effect","sec":"angular-s048","q":"Where can you create an <code>effect</code>, and why?","a":"<p>In an injection context, such as a constructor, a field initializer, or a function run through <code>runInInjectionContext</code>. The effect needs the injector to register with the component or service lifecycle and clean itself up when it is destroyed. Outside that context, you pass an injector in the options, or you get an error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0193","card":"q-angular-047-effect","sec":"angular-s048","q":"When does an effect run for the first time and when after?","a":"<p>It runs at least once, scheduled by Angular, and then again whenever one of the signals it read during the last run changes. Effects run asynchronously, and not immediately when you call <code>set</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0194","card":"q-angular-047-effect","sec":"angular-s048","q":"Which signal reads inside an effect create dependencies?","a":"<p>Any signal read by calling it during the effect function, such as <code>this.count()</code>. A value read outside the function or inside <code>untracked(() =&gt; ...)</code> does not create a dependency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0195","card":"q-angular-048-async-pipe","sec":"angular-s049","q":"What does the <code>async</code> pipe do, and which problem does it solve?","a":"<p>It subscribes to an Observable or Promise in the template, returns the latest emitted value, triggers change detection when new values arrive and unsubscribes when the component is destroyed. It avoids manual subscriptions and forgotten unsubscribes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0196","card":"q-angular-048-async-pipe","sec":"angular-s049","q":"How would you handle several observables with one block in a template?","a":"<p>Wrap them in an object so each is subscribed once: <code>*ngIf=\"{ users: users$ | async, posts: posts$ | async } as data\"</code>. Always true because an object is truthy, and <code>data.users</code> and <code>data.posts</code> are available inside. It is a common trick, though combining them in the component with <code>combineLatest</code> can be cleaner.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0197","card":"q-angular-048-async-pipe","sec":"angular-s049","q":"What does the async pipe return before the observable has emitted?","a":"<p><code>null</code>. This is why templates use <code>?.</code> or an <code>else</code> template for loading states, and why the <code>*ngIf</code> guard works as a loading switch.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0198","card":"q-angular-049-rxjs-and-observables","sec":"angular-s050","q":"What is an Observable, and how does it differ from a Promise?","a":"<p>An Observable is a stream that can emit many values over time and is lazy, because nothing runs until you subscribe. It can be cancelled by unsubscribing and has many operators. A Promise is eager, resolves once with one value and cannot be cancelled.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0199","card":"q-angular-049-rxjs-and-observables","sec":"angular-s050","q":"What do <code>map</code>, <code>filter</code>, <code>tap</code>, <code>switchMap</code> and <code>catchError</code> do?","a":"<p><code>map</code> transforms each value. <code>filter</code> lets through only values that pass a test. <code>tap</code> runs a side effect, such as logging, without changing the value. <code>switchMap</code> maps each value to an inner Observable and cancels the previous inner one. <code>catchError</code> handles an error and returns a fallback Observable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0200","card":"q-angular-049-rxjs-and-observables","sec":"angular-s050","q":"When would you use <code>switchMap</code>, <code>mergeMap</code>, <code>concatMap</code> or <code>exhaustMap</code>?","a":"<p><code>switchMap</code> for the latest request wins, such as type-ahead search. <code>mergeMap</code> to run inner requests in parallel and keep all results. <code>concatMap</code> to run them one after another in order. <code>exhaustMap</code> to ignore new triggers while one is running, such as a submit button.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0201","card":"q-angular-049-rxjs-and-observables","sec":"angular-s050","q":"Why do you need to unsubscribe from some Observables and not from others?","a":"<p>A subscription to an Observable that never completes, such as an interval, a router event or a form value stream, keeps running and holds memory after the component is gone. <code>HttpClient</code> observables complete after one response, so they need no unsubscribe. Use the <code>async</code> pipe, <code>takeUntilDestroyed()</code> or <code>take(1)</code> to stay safe.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0202","card":"q-angular-049-rxjs-and-observables","sec":"angular-s050","q":"What does <code>pipe()</code> do on an Observable?","a":"<p>It chains operators, passing the output of each one to the next, and returns a new Observable. The original is unchanged, and nothing runs until you subscribe.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0203","card":"q-angular-049-rxjs-and-observables","sec":"angular-s050","q":"What is the difference between a cold and a hot Observable?","a":"<p>A cold Observable, such as one from <code>HttpClient.get</code>, starts its work per subscriber, so two subscribers cause two requests. A hot one, such as a <code>Subject</code> or DOM events, produces values regardless of subscribers, and subscribers share them. <code>shareReplay</code> can turn a cold stream into a shared one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0204","card":"q-angular-049-rxjs-and-observables","sec":"angular-s050","q":"When is a <code>BehaviorSubject</code> useful?","a":"<p>It holds a current value and replays it to every new subscriber, which makes it a simple store for state that many components read. Updating it with <code>next(value)</code> notifies all subscribers. Signals now cover many of these local state cases.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0205","card":"q-angular-049-rxjs-and-observables","sec":"angular-s050","q":"Why use <code>catchError</code> in a service pipeline, and where do you put it?","a":"<p>An error ends an Observable, and an uncaught one in an <code>async</code> pipe breaks the view. Place <code>catchError</code> after the HTTP call to log, show a message, or return a fallback such as <code>of([])</code>, so the stream stays alive for the consumer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0206","card":"q-angular-050-unit-testing-basics","sec":"angular-s051","q":"What are Jasmine and Karma, and what role does each play?","a":"<p>Jasmine is the testing framework: it provides <code>describe</code>, <code>it</code>, <code>expect</code>, matchers and spies. Karma is the test runner: it builds the app, opens a browser and runs the specs there, reporting results. They were the Angular CLI default for years. Newer Angular releases are moving to other runners, so check the current docs for the project's version.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0207","card":"q-angular-050-unit-testing-basics","sec":"angular-s051","q":"What do <code>describe</code>, <code>it</code> and <code>beforeEach</code> do in a Jasmine test?","a":"<p><code>describe</code> groups related specs into a suite. <code>it</code> defines one spec with a label that usually starts with \"should\". <code>beforeEach</code> runs before every spec, so each test starts with fresh objects and does not depend on others.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0208","card":"q-angular-050-unit-testing-basics","sec":"angular-s051","q":"What is the Arrange-Act-Assert pattern, and how does it help?","a":"<p>Set up inputs (arrange), run the code (act), then check the result (assert). Separating these steps makes tests readable and shows what each test verifies. Using clearly named <code>expected</code> and <code>actual</code> values makes failures easier to read.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0209","card":"q-angular-050-unit-testing-basics","sec":"angular-s051","q":"What is the difference between <code>toBe</code> and <code>toEqual</code> in Jasmine?","a":"<p><code>toBe</code> checks strict identity (<code>===</code>), so two separate objects with the same content fail. <code>toEqual</code> compares deeply by value, so it suits arrays and objects. Use <code>toBe</code> for primitives.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0210","card":"q-angular-050-unit-testing-basics","sec":"angular-s051","q":"Why does a test failure show expected and actual values?","a":"<p>Jasmine reports both, with the stack trace, so you can see how the result differs from the expectation. This points straight to the faulty logic or an outdated expectation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0211","card":"q-angular-051-testing-without-di-pipes-pure-functions","sec":"angular-s052","q":"Why create the pipe in <code>beforeEach</code> and not once for the whole suite?","a":"<p>A new instance for every spec means no state leaks between tests, so they can run in any order. For stateless pipes the risk is low, but the habit prevents hard-to-find failures.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0212","card":"q-angular-052-testing-with-di-services","sec":"angular-s053","q":"How do you test an Angular service that depends on <code>HttpClient</code> without calling a real server?","a":"<p>Configure <code>TestBed</code> with the testing HTTP support (<code>HttpClientTestingModule</code>, or <code>provideHttpClient()</code> with <code>provideHttpClientTesting()</code> in newer versions), get the service with <code>TestBed.inject</code>, and either control responses with <code>HttpTestingController</code> or replace the method with a spy that returns <code>of(data)</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0213","card":"q-angular-052-testing-with-di-services","sec":"angular-s053","q":"What does <code>spyOn(httpClient, 'get').and.returnValue(of(data))</code> do in a service test?","a":"<p>It replaces <code>httpClient.get</code> with a spy that returns an Observable emitting <code>data</code> immediately, so the service code runs against known data with no network. You can also check how the spy was called.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0214","card":"q-angular-052-testing-with-di-services","sec":"angular-s053","q":"Why do tests for Observable-returning methods take a <code>done</code> callback?","a":"<p>The assertion runs inside <code>subscribe</code>, which may run after the test function returns. Jasmine's <code>done</code> tells it to wait until <code>done()</code> is called. Without it, the test could finish before the assertions run, and pass without checking anything.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0215","card":"q-angular-052-testing-with-di-services","sec":"angular-s053","q":"What happens to a test that subscribes inside an <code>it</code> and never calls <code>done</code>?","a":"<p>Jasmine waits for <code>done</code> and fails the spec with a timeout after the default interval, which is 5 seconds. If the spec does not use <code>done</code>, a failing assertion inside the callback may not be reported at all.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0216","card":"q-angular-052-testing-with-di-services","sec":"angular-s053","q":"Why call <code>TestBed.configureTestingModule</code> in <code>beforeEach</code>?","a":"<p>Each spec then gets a fresh module and fresh service instances, so state does not leak between tests. <code>TestBed</code> resets itself after each spec.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0217","card":"q-angular-053-testing-with-di-services","sec":"angular-s053","q":"How should you structure tests for a service with several methods?","a":"<p>Use a top-level <code>describe</code> for the service and nested <code>describe</code> blocks per method, with one <code>it</code> per behavior. Cover success paths and failure paths such as not found or an empty result.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0218","card":"q-angular-053-testing-with-di-services","sec":"angular-s053","q":"Why use <code>HttpTestingController</code> instead of a spy on <code>HttpClient.get</code>?","a":"<p>It works at the HTTP backend level, so the real <code>HttpClient</code> code and interceptors run. You can <code>expectOne(url)</code>, check method and headers, and <code>flush</code> a response or an error, which tests the service the way it is really called. A spy is simpler but bypasses the HTTP layer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0219","card":"q-angular-054-code-linting-eslint","sec":"angular-s054","q":"What is linting, and what does ESLint catch that the TypeScript compiler does not?","a":"<p>Linting is static analysis of code style and likely mistakes without running it. ESLint finds problems like unused variables, unsafe patterns and inconsistent style, and with Angular plugins it checks conventions such as selector naming. The compiler checks types, but not team style or many bug patterns.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0220","card":"q-angular-054-code-linting-eslint","sec":"angular-s054","q":"How do you add linting to an Angular project?","a":"<p>Run <code>ng lint</code>. If no linter is configured, the CLI offers to add <code>angular-eslint</code> and creates the configuration. After that, <code>npm run lint</code> checks the code. In recent versions the CLI generates a flat <code>eslint.config.js</code>, while older versions use <code>.eslintrc.json</code>, so the file format depends on your version.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0221","card":"q-angular-054-code-linting-eslint","sec":"angular-s054","q":"Which Angular-specific lint rules enforce selector naming, and how do you configure them?","a":"<p><code>@angular-eslint/component-selector</code> and <code>@angular-eslint/directive-selector</code>. Each takes a type (<code>element</code> or <code>attribute</code>), a prefix such as <code>app</code> and a style (<code>kebab-case</code> for components, <code>camelCase</code> for directive attributes). A component with <code>selector: 'appRoot'</code> fails the rule, and <code>'app-root'</code> passes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"angular-f0222","card":"q-angular-054-code-linting-eslint","sec":"angular-s054","q":"Why run lint in CI and before commits?","a":"<p>It stops style problems and common bugs from entering the main branch, and makes code reviews focus on design. A failing lint step blocks the merge, and a pre-commit hook gives faster feedback.</p>","hand":true}];
